        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">When You Can Start</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Walking is the backbone of recovery from a total hip replacement. The AAOS's patient
          exercise guide says proper walking is the best way to help your hip recover, and in the
          same guide it notes that walking on a treadmill is safe if you are concerned about walking
          outside on uneven ground. That makes a treadmill a natural tool for many people. It also
          brings a few hip-specific questions that general treadmill advice does not cover: what your
          precautions mean on a moving belt, how to step on and off without bending or twisting the
          hip, and how to stop a limp becoming a habit. This guide answers them in general terms. Your
          surgeon's and physical therapist's instructions always come first.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#timeline">A Typical Timeline, Week by Week</a></li>
            <li><a href="#precautions">Hip Precautions and What They Mean on a Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#on-and-off">Stepping On and Off Carefully</a></li>
            <li><a href="#first-session">Your First Treadmill Sessions</a></li>
            <li><a href="#gait">Walking Evenly: Gait Symmetry on the Belt</a></li>
            <li><a href="#bike">Bike, Treadmill or Both?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#pain-swelling">Pain, Swelling and the Next-Morning Check</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warning-signs">Warning Signs: When to Stop and Call Your Surgeon</a></li>
            <li><a href="#progression">Building Up After the First Weeks</a></li>
            <li><a href="#long-term">Long Term: Walking and the Running Question</a></li>
            <li><a href="#choosing-a-machine">What to Look For in a Treadmill for Hip Recovery</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Treadmill walking is generally considered a good, low-impact activity after a hip
            replacement, and most people start once they are walking steadily on the floor with a cane
            or without an aid, and their surgeon or therapist has agreed.</strong> Many people reach
            that point within the first several weeks, but recovery speeds vary widely, so your own
            plan sets the date.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>First weeks:</strong> a walker or crutches, with the amount of weight on the
            leg set by your surgeon. The AAOS suggests walking for 5 to 10 minutes, 3 to 4 times a day,
            in the beginning.</li>
            <li><strong>Moving to a cane:</strong> the AAOS says you can begin with a single crutch or
            cane when you can walk and stand for more than 10 minutes without carrying weight on the
            walker or crutches.</li>
            <li><strong>On the treadmill:</strong> flat deck, slow belt, short sessions, rails within
            reach, and no turning or pivoting on the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Precautions:</strong> if you were given hip precautions, they apply on the
            treadmill too, especially when stepping on and off. These differ by surgical approach and
            by surgeon.</li>
            <li><strong>Long term:</strong> walk as much as you like. The AAOS lists jogging among the
            high-impact activities to avoid; ask your surgeon before any running.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Typical Timeline, Week by Week</h2>
<p>
            The table draws together the pattern described by the AAOS's OrthoInfo pages, Hospital for
            Special Surgery (HSS) patient guidance and published hospital rehab protocols. The bands
            overlap deliberately. Your age, fitness before surgery, the surgical approach, the type of
            implant fixation and any weight-bearing limits your surgeon has set can all move you earlier
            or later, and none of that means you are doing it wrong.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical period</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Walking</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Other machines</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Hospital to about week 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walker or crutches; weight on the leg as your surgeon instructs; short walks, often 5 to 10 minutes several times a day (AAOS)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Not usually</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">None at home unless your therapist says so</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About weeks 2 to 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Many move to a cane; HSS describes the first two to four weeks as mainly about recovery</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Sometimes started, slow and flat, once steady with a cane and cleared</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Stationary bike often introduced; one hospital protocol starts it at about 3 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About weeks 6 to 12</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Many walk without an aid; HSS gives single-leg balance for 10 seconds at six weeks as a good goal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Commonly progressed: sessions build toward 20 to 30 minutes at a comfortable pace</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Bike tension increased gradually (the AAOS mentions about 4 to 6 weeks)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About 3 months onward</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Longer walks; strength and balance work continue</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Brisker walking and gentle incline if comfortable</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">HSS says 12 weeks is likely the earliest a surgeon approves a return to sports</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Long term</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walk as much as you like (AAOS)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walking at any comfortable pace and grade; ask before running</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Bike, elliptical and swimming as your surgeon allows</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            One thing the table cannot show is weight-bearing. The AAOS notes that some patients have
            their weight-bearing limited for several weeks after surgery. If you have been told to put
            only partial weight on the leg, a treadmill is not appropriate until that limit is lifted,
            because a moving belt does not let you control how much load each step takes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="precautions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Hip Precautions and What They Mean on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            "Hip precautions" are positions you are asked to avoid for a period after surgery to lower
            the risk of the new joint dislocating. Which ones you get depends mainly on how the
            operation was done, and on your surgeon's own practice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Posterior approach.</strong> The traditional posterior precautions, as summarised by
            HSS, include not bending the hip past 90 degrees, not turning the leg inward more than a
            small amount, using a raised seat and a grabber for things on the floor, and sleeping on
            your back for six weeks. The AAOS's activities page, for patients given posterior
            precautions, adds not crossing the legs at the knees for at least 6 to 8 weeks and not
            turning the feet excessively inward or outward when bending down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anterior approach.</strong> HSS notes that the anterior approach does not involve
            the standard hip precautions, and a clinical review on the US National Library of Medicine's
            StatPearls site says precautions are not generally prescribed after the direct anterior
            approach. Some surgeons still give anterior-approach patients a few limits of their own,
            typically around stretching the leg far behind the body or turning it outward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Practice is changing.</strong> An HSS study of posterior-approach patients found no
            meaningful difference in early dislocation rates between those given a single position to
            avoid and a matched group following the full standard precautions. The authors were clear
            that it was not a randomised trial and that patients with risk factors for instability were
            a separate case. The practical upshot: two people with the same operation can be given
            different rules, and the right rules are the ones your surgeon gave you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How that applies on a treadmill.</strong> Ordinary walking on a flat belt does not
            usually come close to 90 degrees of hip bend, so the walking itself is rarely the problem.
            The risky moments are the ones around it:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bending down</strong> to plug in the machine, pick up a fallen safety key,
            tie a shoelace or grab a water bottle from the floor. Set everything up at waist height
            before you start, and use your grabber if something drops.</li>
            <li><strong>Turning around on the deck,</strong> or twisting your body to look behind you
            or talk to someone, with your foot planted on the belt. That rotation on a fixed foot is the
            kind of movement precautions are meant to prevent.</li>
            <li><strong>Long, reaching strides</strong> at a speed that is too fast, which push the leg
            far behind you at the end of each step. That is worth avoiding whichever approach you had.</li>
            <li><strong>Sitting on the deck or a low step</strong> to rest, which can bend the hip well
            past 90 degrees. If you need to sit, use a firm, high chair placed next to the machine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="on-and-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Stepping On and Off Carefully</h2>
<p>
            For a recovering hip, getting on and off the machine is the part that needs most thought.
            Most home treadmills have a deck several inches above the floor, so every session starts
            and ends with a step up and a step down.
          </p>
          <p>
            The AAOS's guidance on stairs is a helpful model: lead up with your good leg and down with
            your operated leg, using a handrail for balance. On a treadmill that means:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Getting on:</strong> hold both handrails, step up onto the side rails (not the
            belt) with your stronger leg first, then bring the operated leg up beside it. Clip on the
            safety key. Start the belt at its lowest speed, then step onto it, again stronger leg first,
            facing straight ahead. If your machine's manual says to start on a stationary belt, follow
            the manual.</li>
            <li><strong>Getting off:</strong> slow the belt to its lowest speed or stop it completely.
            Step onto the side rails, wait for the belt to stop, then step down with your operated leg
            first, holding the rails. Do not turn round on the deck; if the machine is placed so you must
            face a certain way to get off, move the machine rather than pivoting.</li>
            <li><strong>Never step off a moving belt</strong> at walking speed, and never jump off. Use
            the stop button or the safety key.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-get-on-and-off-a-treadmill-safely/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to get on and off a treadmill safely</a> covers hand positions and what to do if you lose balance. Placement matters too:
            leave clear space behind the machine and enough room beside it for a high chair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-session" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Your First Treadmill Sessions</h2>
<p>
            The AAOS's exercise guide gives a good tone for these sessions: walk as rhythmically and
            smoothly as you can, and don't hurry. Think of the first week or two as practice at walking
            well on a moving surface rather than as exercise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> Start at the lowest speed at which you can walk naturally. On many
            home treadmills the belt starts at 0.5 mph, and around 1 to 1.5 mph is a comfortable first
            setting for many people after hip surgery. If you find yourself taking short, uneven steps to
            keep up, it is too fast. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed for seniors</a> explains how to find a comfortable pace by feel rather than by number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Duration.</strong> Five to ten minutes, in line with the AAOS's suggestion of 5 to
            10 minute walks in the beginning. If your hip feels no worse that evening and the next
            morning, add a couple of minutes next time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> Keep the deck flat for the first few weeks of treadmill use. Uphill
            walking asks more of the hip muscles and tends to exaggerate a limp. Add 1 to 2 percent for
            short blocks only after flat walking is comfortable and your therapist agrees.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Handrails.</strong> Hold them lightly for balance. Early in recovery that is exactly
            what they are for; the usual advice to walk hands-free is aimed at healthy exercisers. What
            you want to avoid is leaning heavily on the rails to unload the hip. Our explainer on <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">holding the handrails on a treadmill</a> covers when holding on helps and when it gets in the way.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Shoes and supervision.</strong> Wear supportive, closed-toe shoes that you can put on
            without bending past your limits, using a long-handled shoehorn if needed. For the first few
            sessions, have someone at home who knows you are on the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gait" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Evenly: Gait Symmetry on the Belt</h2>
<p>
            One of the most common problems after a hip replacement is a limp that hangs on after the
            pain has gone. The muscles on the outside of the hip, which keep your pelvis level as you
            stand on one leg, are often weak after surgery and after years of arthritis beforehand. When
            they cannot hold the pelvis, the body leans over the operated side with each step, or you
            take a quicker, shorter step on that leg.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill is actually quite good for working on this, because the belt gives you a steady,
            even rhythm to match and you can watch yourself in a mirror or window. Some things to aim for,
            which your physical therapist can refine:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Equal step length.</strong> Listen to your footfalls; they should sound evenly
            spaced. An uneven rhythm usually means a shorter step on one side.</li>
            <li><strong>Equal time on each leg.</strong> Try not to hurry off the operated leg.</li>
            <li><strong>A level pelvis and upright trunk.</strong> Avoid leaning toward the operated
            side as you step onto it.</li>
            <li><strong>Heel to toe.</strong> Land on the heel and roll through, rather than walking
            flat-footed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you cannot walk evenly at a given speed, slow down until you can. A slow, even walk
            trains a better pattern than a faster, lopsided one. HSS points out that walking is also a
            balance exercise, because it involves shifting your weight from one leg to the other, so
            these sessions help your stability as well as your stamina. Keep up the hip-strengthening
            exercises your therapist gave you; they are what make an even walk possible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bike" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bike, Treadmill or Both?</h2>
<p>
            The AAOS's exercise guide calls riding an <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>exercise bike</a> an excellent activity to help you
            regain muscle strength and hip mobility, and its activities page notes that your doctor may
            recommend a stationary bicycle, typically at physical therapy. One hospital rehab protocol,
            from Mass General Brigham, lists both stationary cycling and treadmill walking as endurance
            work after hip replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            For people with posterior precautions, the bike needs one specific adjustment. The AAOS
            suggests setting the seat high at first, so the bottom of your foot just touches the pedal
            with your knee almost straight. A seat that is too low bends the hip sharply at the top of
            each pedal stroke. The guide also suggests pedalling backwards first and adding resistance as
            you get stronger, at about 4 to 6 weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            A recumbent bike can be easier to get on and off, though it places you in a more seated
            position, so check with your therapist about seat setting if you have flexion limits. Our
            guide to the <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best recumbent bikes</a> looks at step-through heights and seat ranges. In practice, many people use the bike to work
            on mobility and the treadmill to practise walking, once both are cleared.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pain-swelling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Pain, Swelling and the Next-Morning Check</h2>
<p>
            Some soreness and swelling is a normal part of recovery, especially in the thigh. What you
            are watching for is whether the treadmill is moving you forward or setting you back.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>During the walk:</strong> a mild ache that stays steady is generally acceptable.
            Sharp pain, pain that builds, or a limp that worsens means slow down or stop.</li>
            <li><strong>That evening:</strong> some extra soreness that settles with rest is common.</li>
            <li><strong>Next morning:</strong> if the hip and thigh feel no worse than before the
            session, the amount was about right. If they are clearly more painful, stiffer or swollen,
            cut the next session back by about a third or take a day off.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Ask your therapist whether this kind of rule suits you; many use something similar. A short
            log of minutes, speed and how you felt the next morning makes the decision easy and gives
            your therapist something concrete to review.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warning Signs: When to Stop and Call Your Surgeon</h2>
<p>
            The AAOS's guidance on activities after hip replacement lists signs you should report to
            your doctor straight away. Walking more often coincides with noticing them, so know them
            before you start.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Possible blood clot in the leg:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Pain in your leg or calf that is not related to your incision</li>
            <li>Severe swelling of your thigh, calf, ankle or foot that does not go away when you
            elevate your leg</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Possible infection:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Persistent fever higher than 100°F</li>
            <li>Drainage from your wound</li>
            <li>Increasing redness, tenderness or swelling of the wound</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Call 911 or your local emergency number</strong> for sudden shortness of breath or
            chest pain that comes on suddenly. The AAOS lists these as possible signs of a clot in the
            lungs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Possible dislocation or injury.</strong> Stop and get medical help at once if you
            feel or hear a clunk or pop followed by severe pain, cannot put weight on the leg, or notice
            that the leg suddenly looks shorter or is turned in or out. The same applies after any fall.
            Do not try to walk it off or put the hip back yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="progression" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Building Up After the First Weeks</h2>
<p>
            HSS's guidance on exercise after hip replacement puts it simply: you should not go from
            walking one block to walking twenty. It describes the first two to four weeks as mainly about
            recovery, with a return to a more structured routine often around four to six weeks. On the
            treadmill, change one thing at a time and give each change about a week.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Time first:</strong> add a few minutes per session up to 20 to 30 minutes.
            Two shorter walks a day are fine.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed second:</strong> 0.1 or 0.2 mph at a time, only while your stride stays
            even.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline last:</strong> 1 to 2 percent in short blocks, then a little more as
            comfort allows.</li>
            <li><strong>Hands last of all:</strong> move from two hands on the rails to one, then to a
            light touch, as your balance improves.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a ready-made gentle structure, our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout for seniors</a> and our guide to <a href="/how-long-should-seniors-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long seniors should walk on a treadmill</a> both use time-based steps that suit anyone returning from surgery. If your other hip or your
            knees are arthritic, our page on <a href="/treadmill-walking-with-arthritis/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking with arthritis</a> covers pacing a walk around sore joints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-term" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Long Term: Walking and the Running Question</h2>
<p>
            The AAOS's activities page says to walk as much as you would like after a hip replacement,
            while remembering that walking does not replace your prescribed exercises. It describes
            lower-impact activities such as golfing, bicycling, doubles tennis and pickleball as putting
            less stress on the hip, and swimming as an excellent option once the wound has healed.
          </p>
          <p>
            On running, sources differ in tone. The AAOS advises against high-impact activities including
            jogging, singles tennis, racquetball, basketball and skiing. HSS's exercise guidance is more
            open, saying more vigorous activities such as running are possible for most people, but not in
            the near term, and warning that the more demand you put on the replacement, the less time it
            is likely to last. Both point to the same practical answer: running is a conversation with your
            surgeon, not a decision to make on your own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking still leaves plenty of room for a real workout: a brisk pace on a moderate incline
            raises your heart rate without the impact of running. If you are deciding whether a treadmill
            is the right long-term machine, our guide on <a href="/is-a-treadmill-good-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill is good for seniors</a> compares it with bikes and ellipticals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing-a-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Look For in a Treadmill for Hip Recovery</h2>
<p>
            The useful features are the same ones that make any treadmill easier for an unsteady walker,
            with a little extra weight on getting on and off.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A low step-up height,</strong> so the step onto the deck asks less of the hip.</li>
            <li><strong>Long side handrails</strong> you can hold without reaching forward or twisting.</li>
            <li><strong>A 0.5 mph starting speed</strong> with 0.1 mph steps.</li>
            <li><strong>Controls at hand height,</strong> so nothing requires bending down once you are on.</li>
            <li><strong>A stable, non-bouncy deck</strong> and a clip-on safety key.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our picks of the <a href="/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking machines for seniors</a> prioritise low speeds, low decks and full-length rails, and our roundup of <a href="/best-treadmills-with-handrails/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with long handrails</a> lists models with rails along both sides; our guide to <a href="/ksports-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Ksports treadmills</a> covers a budget
            rehab walking treadmill with removable long rails. Most walking pads have no rails and a remote control
            that is easy to drop; our guide to <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for seniors</a> covers the few with a support bar, which are better suited to later in recovery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Treadmill walking is a good fit after a hip replacement once you are steady on the
            floor, any weight-bearing limits have been lifted, and your surgeon or therapist has agreed.</strong> The AAOS describes treadmill walking as safe if you are worried about uneven ground outside.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep your hip precautions on the machine as well as off it: set everything up at waist height,
            step on with the stronger leg and off with the operated leg, and never turn or pivot on the
            belt. Start flat, slow and short, walk evenly rather than quickly, and let the next-morning
            check guide your progress. Learn the warning signs before you begin, and talk to your surgeon
            before any running.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes on the Treadmill After Hip Replacement</h2>
          <p>Most problems happen around the walk rather than during it: getting on and off, bending down, turning, and doing too much too soon.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Turning around on the belt</h3>
              <p>Pivoting on a planted foot, or twisting to look behind you, rotates the hip in exactly the way many precautions are designed to avoid. Stop the belt, step onto the side rails, and get off facing forward. If the machine's position makes that awkward, move the machine.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Bending down mid-session</h3>
              <p>A dropped safety key, phone or bottle tempts you to reach for the floor, which can bend the hip past 90 degrees. Set everything up at waist height before you start, keep a grabber nearby, and stop the belt before picking anything up.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Walking fast with a limp</h3>
              <p>Speed that forces short, uneven steps on the operated leg trains a limp rather than fixing it. Slow down until your footfalls sound evenly spaced and your trunk stays upright, then build speed only as fast as your stride stays even.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Starting while weight-bearing is still limited</h3>
              <p>If your surgeon has restricted how much weight you put on the leg, a treadmill is not the place to practise, because the belt does not let you control the load. Wait until the restriction is lifted and your therapist agrees.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Walking on a treadmill after a hip replacement is generally safe
            and useful once you are steady on the floor, cleared for full weight-bearing, and your
            surgeon or therapist agrees.</strong> Start flat, slow and short.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep your hip precautions on the machine: no bending to the floor, no turning on the belt, on
            with the stronger leg and off with the operated one. A machine with <a href="/best-treadmills-with-handrails/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">long handrails</a> and a low deck makes all of that easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
