        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>When can I walk on a treadmill after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually once you are walking steadily on level ground with a cane or without an aid, any weight-bearing limits have been lifted, and your surgeon or physical therapist agrees. For many people that is within the first several weeks, but timelines vary widely. The AAOS says treadmill walking is safe if you are concerned about uneven ground outside.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is walking on a treadmill good after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Generally, yes. The AAOS calls proper walking the best way to help your hip recover and says treadmill walking is safe, and HSS lists treadmills among excellent low-impact options. The belt gives an even surface and a steady rhythm for working on a symmetrical walk. Start slow and flat, and follow any hip precautions you were given.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do hip precautions apply on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, if you were given them. Flat walking rarely bends the hip past 90 degrees, but bending down to pick things up, sitting on the deck, and turning or pivoting on a planted foot can all break posterior precautions. Precautions differ by surgical approach and surgeon; anterior-approach patients often have few or none, so follow the rules your surgeon gave you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I use on a treadmill after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start at the slowest speed that lets you walk naturally, often around 1 to 1.5 mph on a flat deck for five to ten minutes. Raise it by 0.1 or 0.2 mph at a time only while your steps stay even on both sides. If you start limping or taking short steps on the operated leg, slow down.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I get on and off a treadmill after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Hold the rails, step up onto the side rails with your stronger leg first, clip on the safety key and start the belt slowly before stepping on. To get off, stop or slow the belt, step onto the side rails, wait for the belt to stop, then step down with your operated leg first. Never turn around on the deck.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I run on a treadmill after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Ask your surgeon first. The AAOS advises against high-impact activities such as jogging after a hip replacement. HSS says running may be possible for many people eventually, but not in the near term, and that more demand on the joint may shorten its life. Brisk walking with a gentle incline is the usual long-term treadmill workout.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warning signs should I watch for when walking after hip replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The AAOS lists leg or calf pain not related to the incision and severe swelling that does not go down with elevation as possible clot signs, and fever over 100°F, wound drainage or increasing redness as possible infection signs. Call emergency services for sudden breathlessness or chest pain, and get help at once for a pop with severe pain or inability to bear weight.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

